, while patterns are minimalist (root notes on off‑beats or 1/8–1/16 pulses) and the kick/bass relationship is controlled with **sidechain ducking of 3–6 dB**, short attacks, and musical tuning of the kick to the track key.[1][2][11][15]\n\nBelow is a structured rundown addressing each part of your question, with URLs where available from the sources I retrieved earlier.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Splitting sub and bass roles\n\n**Conceptual split**\n\nMost techno/dub techno workflows follow this basic division:\n\n- **Sub layer**\n  - Pure sine or near‑sine.\n  - Plays the **root note**, often with very simple rhythms (half‑notes, quarter‑notes, or off‑beat pulses).\n  - Lives in the **40–80 Hz** region; strongest fundamental usually around **45–60 Hz** for 128–135 BPM techno.[11][15]\n  - Kept **mono** and very controlled in dynamics and timing.\n\n- **Bass / mid‑bass layer**\n  - Carries **character, movement and harmonics** (saturation, filter movement, chorus, etc.).\n  - Often occupies **80–250 Hz** and up into the low mids.\n  - May have more syncopation or 1/16‑note style motion; often filtered so it doesn’t fight the kick fundamental.\n\n**Examples from sources**\n\n- Attack Magazine’s Basic Channel‑style dub techno tutorial uses a **single sub sample** tuned to two notes (D# and G#) on separate Drum Rack pads, both routed through the same sidechain compressor so they duck with the kick.[1]  \n  URL:  \n  https://www.attackmagazine.com/technique/beat-dissected/basic-channel-style-dub-techno/\n\n- A warehouse‑style rolling techno bass article on Attack focuses on an **“isolated sub”** (low‑passed around 80 Hz) plus **overdrive** for harmonics, implying a clear separation between sub and higher bass content.[2]  \n  URL:  \n  https://www.attackmagazine.com/technique/tutorials/warehouse-rolling-techno-bass/\n\n- Techno mixdown guides emphasise **one owner of the sub** (kick or bass, not both), and keeping other bass information a bit higher or sidechained.[11][15]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Typical note patterns for hypnotic/dub techno\n\n**Rhythmic roles**\n\nFor hypnotic/dub techno, bass and sub generally aim for **repetition and subtle variation** rather than melodic complexity:\n\n- **Root pulses on off‑beats**\n  - Common pattern: **root note on the “and” of each beat** (between kicks) to create a call‑and‑response with the 4‑on‑the‑floor kick.\n  - This is especially typical when the kick dominates the sub; the bass hits when the kick decays.\n\n- **Straight 1/8 or 1/16 patterns**\n  - Many rolling warehouse/peak‑time patterns use **constant 1/8 notes**, sometimes with small rhythmic variations (occasional rests, ghost notes, or one note shifted to create tension).\n  - Dub techno often slows this down—**longer sustained notes**, sometimes synced to 1/2 or 1‑bar lengths, with delay and filter modulation giving movement instead of busy note patterns.[1][2]\n\n- **Two‑note riffs**\n  - The Basic Channel‑style example uses **two notes (D# and G#)** over a simple programmable pattern, giving a subtly shifting but still hypnotic line.[1]  \n\n**Melodic content**\n\n- Bass lines are usually **root‑centric**, occasionally hitting the 5th or another chord tone, but rarely walking through complex scales.\n- Movement is more about **rhythmic placement, decay, and filter modulation** than about melodic lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Keeping kick and bass from clashing\n\nThis is handled by a combination of **frequency, time, and level management**.\n\n#### 3.1 Frequency management and tuning\n\n- **Kick “owns” a specific low band**\n  - A techno mixdown checklist states that at **128–135 BPM the kick fundamental typically sits around 45–55 Hz**, and “nothing else is allowed to live there.”[11]  \n    URL:  \n    https://tracksensei.com/blog/techno-mixdown-checklist\n  - Bass is either **tucked above** that range or **ducked** whenever the kick hits.\n\n- **Low‑pass / high‑pass shaping**\n  - Attack’s warehouse bass tutorial low‑passes the sub around **80 Hz** with a 12 dB/oct filter, focusing its energy in that band.[2]\n  - Other low‑end guides recommend high‑passing non‑essential instruments so the kick/sub are not masked.[5][11][15]\n\n- **Tuning the kick to the track key**\n  - Low‑end mixing resources (including iZotope and production tutorials) recommend **tuning the kick** so that its fundamental matches the **root note of the track** or a consonant interval (root or fifth), especially when the sub plays sustained notes.[5][9][11][15]\n  - This reduces perceived clashes because the **kick and bass are harmonically aligned** rather than hitting random notes.\n\n#### 3.2 Sidechain compression / ducking\n\nWhile sources don’t all give identical numbers, they converge around similar **ballpark settings**.\n\n**Gain reduction**\n\n- A techno mixdown checklist suggests **3–6 dB of gain reduction** on the bass when the kick hits, using sidechain compression from the kick.[11]\n- iZotope’s tutorials on mixing kick and bass also show settings where the **low band of the bass ducks a few dB** when the kick hits.[5][8][9]  \n  Example article (kick/bass):  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/how-to-mix-kick-and-bass  \n  Low‑end mixing overview:  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/7-tips-for-mixing-the-low-end  \n\n**Attack**\n\n- For clean techno, the goal is usually:\n  - **Fast attack** on the bass sidechain so the bass ducks before it masks the kick transient.\n  - Many mixing tutorials recommend attack values in the **1–10 ms** range for this purpose; iZotope and Neutron examples show relatively fast attacks when ducking low end.[5][9][12][14]\n- Shorter attack means the **kick transient and initial thump are clearly heard** before the bass comes back up.\n\n**Release**\n\n- Techno mixdown examples suggest:\n  - Release short enough that **bass returns before the next bass note and before the next kick**, but not so short that pumping becomes obvious:\n  - A techno checklist specifically mentions **release short enough that the bass is back at full level before the next quarter note** at typical techno tempos.[11]\n- In practical terms at ~128 BPM:\n  - A quarter note is about 470 ms; many producers place release somewhere in the **80–250 ms** region so you get a smooth dip and recovery before the next kick, depending on how much groove/pump you want.\n\n**Alternative: volume ducking / automation**\n\n- iZotope and other low‑end tutorials mention **volume automation or gain plugins** as an alternative, manually ducking the bass when the kick hits instead of relying on a compressor.[4][6][12]\n- The principle is the same: **3–6 dB dips** timed around the kick transient, with curves matching the groove.\n\n#### 3.3 Band‑limited sidechain and dynamic EQ\n\n- iZotope demonstrates **multiband sidechain compression** where only the **sub‑bass region (below ~100 Hz)** of the bass is ducked by the kick, leaving midrange bass harmonics intact.[8][9]  \n  URL example (bass compression with sidechain):  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/bass-compression-part2\n- They also show **dynamic EQ with external sidechain** from the kick, so specific masking frequencies in the bass dip only when the kick hits.[10][14]  \n  Example dynamic EQ article:  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/5-ways-to-use-dynamic-eq-with-sidechain  \n\n---\n\n### 4. Typical sub octave for techno\n\nExact octave depends on tuning and tempo, but production guides point to:\n\n- For 4/4 techno at **128–135 BPM**:\n  - Kick fundamental around **45–55 Hz**, so:\n    - If the kick owns that, the **sub‑bass line often focuses slightly above (e.g., 55–70 Hz) for its main energy** or is sidechained.[11]\n  - Many low‑end guides for electronic genres implicitly place **sub content between 30–80 Hz**, with most usable energy in the **40–60 Hz** band.[5][15]\n- In MIDI terms:\n  - **E1–G1 (~41–49 Hz)** or **A1–C2 (~55–65 Hz)** are common roots for sub in techno, depending on key and system constraints (this follows directly from the frequency ranges above).\n\nSo for practical techno production:\n\n- **Sub‑bass octave**: typically **around 40–70 Hz**, centered near **45–60 Hz**.\n- Bass layers with more character and harmonics often emphasize **60–120 Hz** and above.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Why low frequencies are kept mono (≈100–150 Hz)\n\nMultiple sources explicitly state that **all sub content should be mono**, usually below **100–150 Hz**:\n\n- A techno mixdown checklist recommends making “everything mono below **120–150 Hz**” using a utility or mono‑maker plugin, so the mix survives mono playback and the low end feels solid.[11]\n- Other low‑end guides suggest **“keep all sub content (under ~100 Hz) mono”**, and in Ableton use **Utility’s Bass Mono** set around **100–120 Hz**.[13][15]\n- A techno kick design article stresses that the **low‑end of the kick should almost always be mono**, using a utility plugin to set stereo width to zero.[7]  \n  URL:  \n  https://toolroomacademy.com/features/how-to-make-techno-perfecting-your-kick/\n\n**Technical reasons**\n\n1. **Localization and phase**\n   - Human hearing struggles to localize very low frequencies; stereo width in the sub range mostly comes from **phase differences**, which easily cause cancellation between speakers or in different rooms.\n   - Making the sub mono minimizes **phase cancellation** and ensures consistent low‑end power across playback systems.[5][11][15]\n\n2. **Club and PA compatibility**\n   - Many club systems sum lows to mono or use mono subwoofers; if your low end is already mono, you avoid unexpected changes when the signal is downmixed.[11][15]\n\n3. **Headroom and clarity**\n   - Stereo sub content can **waste headroom** and cause the low end to feel blurry; mono sub concentrates energy in the center and leaves **stereo width to mid and high frequencies**.[5][11][15]\n\n**Typical crossover choices**\n\n- **120 Hz** is a common default:\n  - “Put a stereo utility on your master and set it to mono below 120 Hz… in Ableton Live that is Utility with Bass Mono at 120 Hz; wide detuned bass genres can pull the crossover down to 100 Hz — never below 80 Hz.”[15]\n- Techno‑specific guides:\n  - Recommend **mono below 120–150 Hz**, slightly higher than some EDM/808 genres, to keep the kick and sub rock‑solid in the club context.[11]\n\n---\n\n### 6. Ableton‑ and iZotope‑oriented details\n\n**Ableton Live specifics (from the gathered material)**\n\n- **Making low end mono**\n  - Use **Utility**:\n    - Older versions: set **Width = 0%** for full mono.\n    - Newer versions: use **Bass Mono** with crossover set around **100–120 Hz**, leaving higher bands stereo.[13][15]\n\n- **Sub design in Attack’s dub techno examples**\n  - Place sub samples in **Drum Rack pads** and tune them to desired notes.\n  - Use **short envelopes** and **sidechain compression** from the kick so the sub ducks on kick hits.[1]\n\n**iZotope‑centric workflows**\n\n- **Neutron / Ozone**\n  - Use **Neutron Compressor** or **Multiband Compressor** on bass, with the kick as external sidechain, to duck only the **low band** (e.g., below 100 Hz).[8][9]\n  - Use **Dynamic EQ with Ext. Full** as the sidechain source from the kick, so specific frequencies in the bass duck only when needed.[10][14]\n- **General low‑end tips**\n  - Focus on **20–160 Hz** as the main low‑end region for kick and bass.\n  - Use sidechain instead of just EQ to manage overlap: **sidechain the bass to the kick** so the kick transient is clear.[5]\n\n---\n\n### 7. Practical starting points (translated into numbers)\n\nTo tie this to your question about concrete settings:\n\n- **Pattern ideas for hypnotic/dub techno**\n  - Sub: root note, **off‑beats** or simple **1/8 note pulses**, sometimes sustained for 1–2 beats.\n  - Bass/mid‑bass: 1/8 or 1/16 notes with small rhythmic variations; use filter modulation and subtle saturation instead of density for hypnosis.\n\n- **Kick vs bass**\n  - Pick whether **kick or bass owns 45–55 Hz**; if the kick owns it, tilt bass up or sidechain.\n  - **Sidechain compression on bass**:\n    - Gain reduction: **3–6 dB** per kick hit.[11]\n    - Attack: **1–10 ms** (fast enough to protect the kick transient).\n    - Release: roughly **80–250 ms**; adjust so bass is back before the next kick at your tempo without audible pumping.\n  - Optionally use **band‑limited sidechain** so only sub frequencies duck.\n\n- **Sub octave**\n  - Aim sub fundamentals around **40–60 Hz**.\n  - Align bass note choice so the **kick’s tuned fundamental** is harmonically consonant (root or fifth) with the sub line.\n\n- **Mono low end**\n  - In Ableton: **Utility → Bass Mono = 100–120 Hz**.\n  - Alternatively: any mono‑maker plugin on the master or low‑end bus set around **120 Hz**, rarely lower than **80 Hz**.[11][13][15]\n\n---\n\n### Key URLs from the gathered sources\n\n- Attack Magazine – Basic Channel‑style dub techno (sub riff, sidechain, EQ of ultra‑lows):  \n  https://www.attackmagazine.com/technique/beat-dissected/basic-channel-style-dub-techno/ [1]\n\n- Attack Magazine – Warehouse‑style rolling techno basslines (sub isolation, low‑pass, overdrive):  \n  https://www.attackmagazine.com/technique/tutorials/warehouse-rolling-techno-bass/ [2]\n\n- iZotope – 7 tips for mixing the low end (general low‑end practice, sidechain kick/bass):  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/7-tips-for-mixing-the-low-end [5]\n\n- iZotope – How to mix kick and bass (neutron sidechain, low‑band ducking):  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/how-to-mix-kick-and-bass [9]\n\n- iZotope – Bass compression with kick sidechain (multiband below ~100 Hz):  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/bass-compression-part2 [8]\n\n- iZotope – 5 ways to use dynamic EQ with sidechain:  \n  https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/5-ways-to-use-dynamic-eq-with-sidechain [10][14]\n\n- Techno mixdown checklist (kick at 45–55 Hz, 3–6 dB sidechain, mono below 120–150 Hz):  \n  https://tracksensei.com/blog/techno-mixdown-checklist [11]\n\n- How to mix low end like a pro (mono below 120 Hz, one owner of the sub, genre‑specific notes):  \n  https://www.futuresoundacademy.com/blog/mix-low-end-guide [15]\n\n- Toolroom Academy – Perfecting your techno kick (low‑end of kick mono):  \n  https://toolroomacademy.com/features/how-to-make-techno-perfecting-your-kick [7]\n\nIf you want, I can next help you design a specific dub‑techno style bass pattern (with exact MIDI notes and milliseconds for sidechain settings) tailored to your BPM and key.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.attackmagazine.com/technique/beat-dissected/basic-channel-style-dub-techno/","title":"Basic Channel-Style Dub Techno - Attack Magazine","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.attackmagazine.com/technique/tutorials/warehouse-rolling-techno-bass/","title":"Sculpting Warehouse-Style Rolling Techno Basslines","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AXDY7oJgnzM","title":"Lowend Bass in Mono Tutorial for Techno / Minimal in Ableton Live","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.izotope.com/community/blog/7-tips-for-mixing-the-low-end","title":"7 tips for mixing the low end","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.izotope.com/en/learn/7-tips-for-mixing-the-low-end","title":"7 tips for mixing the low end - iZotope","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EzMfpMZwuRY&vl=en","title":"How To Get A Punchy, Clean Low End (Step By Step)","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://toolroomacademy.com/features/how-to-make-techno-perfecting-your-kick/","title":"How To Make Techno: Perfecting Your Kick","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_